# Backups, restore and recovery copies

Give someone enough information to choose the right recovery material and understand what a restore replaces. A theme-file recovery copy, an archive checksum and a Home Assistant backup answer different questions. None alone proves a working system can be restored.

## Scope and native ownership

Home Assistant provides encrypted backups, storage destinations and native restore workflows. Available contents depend on installation type and selected scope; external databases can require separate protection. Keep the current native backup and recovery documentation authoritative. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/common-tasks/general/backups/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-019**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Requested / creating | Name the backup operation and real progress if supplied. | Do not say Backup ready before creation finishes. |
| Created locally | Show creation time, included scope and local location. | Do not imply a remote copy exists. |
| Upload partly failed | Show each successful and failed destination. | Do not discard a valid local copy or announce all destinations saved. |
| Archive verified | State whether integrity, decryption or a restore was checked. | A hash match is not a successful restore test. |
| Restoring / interrupted | Explain replacement scope and native reconnect expectations. | Do not invent a safe Cancel or a remaining-time estimate. |

## Integration contract

1. Before creating or restoring, identify installation type, selected contents, destination, backup date and any omitted data. Use native permissions and confirmation.
2. Before restore confirmation, state which current configuration/data will be replaced and whether an interruption is expected. Link to the current native procedure rather than scripting a parallel restore.
3. Keep encryption keys, passwords and recovery materials out of example HTML, URLs, screenshots and logs. Provide handling instructions without echoing secrets back to a shared display.
4. Record a selected-file rollback by its exact paths, prior and resulting hashes and preconditions. It must never be labelled a full backup or imply coverage for devices, external databases or unrelated settings.

## Accessible interaction

- Use structured lists of included and excluded contents; do not hide the replacement warning in optional details.
- Announce stage changes, not every progress tick. Provide a textual status when no measured percentage exists.
- Ensure the final confirmation identifies the chosen archive and its date in the accessible name/context; focus should return predictably if it is cancelled before execution.

## Failure and recovery

- A failed destination needs a per-destination retry route where supported; do not recreate a successful backup merely to hide the failure.
- For a decryption error, keep the archive selection and native error; never suggest disabling encryption as the automatic remedy.
- If restore disconnects the browser, follow the native recovery procedure. Closing a dialog or tab must not be described as stopping the restore.

